= #text("S2. Evidence contracts and boundary conventions")

#text("Fix one evidence-bearing task and its finite sentence-ID domain. Its reference family consists of one or more nonempty sets. Several alternatives may support the same task; an output need contain one complete alternative, not the union of all alternatives. In the authored finite certificate, alternatives may overlap. That broader certificate class is explicitly different from claiming that every such family occurs in the native dataset. No independent domain expert adjudicated reference completeness or correctness.")

#text("A and B are ordered lists without duplicate IDs. Empty input lists are admitted in boundary checks, but an empty input cannot be annotation-sufficient when all references are nonempty. A valid generated order likewise contains distinct IDs. For nonnegative integer k, its prefix contains the first min(k,length) entries. Cap zero yields the empty output. Capacities beyond the union size do not pad or invent IDs. An ID outside the output order has infinite rank; the infinity convention concerns absence, not a numeric rank inserted into the response.")

#text("Full containment S asks whether at least one reference is a subset of the entire output. First-three asks the same question after an additional prefix-three operation. Exact-annotated E asks whether the entire output set equals one listed alternative. An empty output scores zero for all three on evidence-bearing tasks. The original broader task audit also has insufficient-evidence tasks with a separate label/empty-evidence contract; those are not relabeled as members of the current 309-task pair bank.")

#text("These endpoints can disagree legitimately. Adding an unannotated ID preserves full containment but can destroy exact matching. Moving a required ID behind position three preserves the full set but can destroy first-three acceptance. An unannotated ID is not automatically semantically irrelevant, and an exact-match failure is not automatically an incorrect answer. Conversely, if the application truly requires a short ranked rationale, its first-three failure is operationally real even when the evidence still occurs later. Correct labels are supplied by the auditor throughout; label prediction, retrieval from a corpus, native aggregate F1, and model faithfulness are outside the executed contract.")

$ S(X) = bb(1)[exists R in cal(R): R subset.eq X] $

$ F_(3) (L) = S("set"(L[1: min(3, |L|)])) $

$ E(X) = bb(1)[exists R in cal(R): X = R] $

= #text("S3. Stable operators and information permissions")

#text("The complete round-robin order V is constructed before the output cap. Visit A's first entry, B's first entry, A's second entry, B's second entry, continuing through the longer list. Append an ID only on its first visit. Thus V contains each element of the union once. Define C as the intersection. P is the C-subsequence of V followed by the non-C subsequence of V. This is a stable partition, not sorting by ID value, reference membership, or an inferred semantic relevance score.")

#text("The policy input is exactly A, B, and k. Reference families are passed only to the scoring/auditing stage. The construction code does not receive reference labels, completed-reference ranks, the both-input-sufficient inclusion flag, or a held-out score. Completion-rank and removed-ID certificates are gold-aware explanations after construction; they are not exposed as routing features. A policy that used those certificates to select its output would have different information rights and could not inherit the label-blind comparison.")

#text("The earlier agreement-first rule obtained the relative order of common IDs from a left-priority construction. The current stable partition obtains both class orders from V. At capacity three, every common ID fits because each input has at most three IDs; set-based endpoint results match the saved priority audit. At capacities one and two, a prefix may contain only some common IDs, so the change in internal class order matters. These small-cap results are not described as an identical rerun of the previous constructor.")

#text("An interleaving, a common-item promotion, truncating an input before union construction, and truncating the completed union are different interventions. Only the last specified sequence is analyzed. An implementation that drops entries from A or B before deduplication, sorts common IDs numerically, consults a reference, or silently fills an empty intersection with oracle IDs changes the experiment. The reproducibility check must compare exact ordered outputs as well as their scores: tied scores alone cannot establish that the intended constructor was executed.")

#method-algorithm(([#text("INPUT A, B, nonnegative integer k; initialize V=[], seen=empty set.")],[#text("For index i from 0 to max(len(A),len(B))-1, visit A[i] then B[i] when present.")],[#text("Append a visited ID to V only if it is not already in seen; then add it to seen.")],[#text("Set C=set(A) intersect set(B).")],[#text("Set P=[x in V with x in C] followed by [x in V with x not in C].")],[#text("Return the prefix of V and the prefix of P of length min(k,len(V)).")],[#text("Only after outputs are frozen, evaluate the original nonempty reference alternatives.")],),"Deterministic label-blind constructor. References are not an argument. The scorer is a separate audit step.")

= #text("S4. Completion ranks and the single-window proof")

#text("Fix any complete distinct-ID order L and nonempty alternative R. If every member of R appears in L, the smallest prefix containing R ends at its last required ID. Its completion rank is therefore the maximum of the member ranks. If at least one member is absent, no finite prefix completes R and that alternative's completion rank is infinity. The family is accepted as soon as its earliest-completing alternative is accepted, so take the minimum over alternatives. This establishes the formula in both the finite and absent-reference cases without assuming equal reference lengths or disjoint alternatives.")

#text("To prove the prefix identity in both directions, suppose k is at least the minimum completion rank. A minimizing finite alternative has every member ranked at most k and is a subset of the prefix, giving sufficiency. Conversely, if the prefix is sufficient, some alternative lies entirely inside it, so its maximum member rank is at most k and the minimum completion rank is no larger. If all alternatives have infinite completion rank, no finite prefix passes. The minimum is attained whenever it is finite because the recorded family is finite.")

#text("Let b and p be the baseline and promoted completion thresholds. If p is below b, the promoted output alone passes exactly for p <= k < b. Before p both fail; from b onward both pass. If b is below p, baseline alone passes exactly for b <= k < p. Equal thresholds give ties at every capacity, including the case where both are infinite. When one threshold is infinite the interval is unbounded above over unrestricted capacities; on the audited finite range it is intersected with zero through six. For two permutations of the same full union, reachability is shared, so either both thresholds are finite or both infinite.")

#text("This proves a single contiguous non-tie interval and a single polarity for each fixed pair under full containment. It does not prove a one-capacity interval. A manually authored teaching example uses A=(1,2,0), B=(4,5,0), but changes the reference family to the singleton alternative {0}. This is not the saved task's original annotation. V=(1,4,2,5,0) has threshold five; P=(0,1,4,2,5) has threshold one. Gains then span capacities one through four. The saved bank's observed width-one intervals are a property of that bank, not a theorem about stable promotion.")

#text("Exact matching lacks the same monotonicity: a prefix can equal an alternative and then stop equaling it after an extra ID is added. First-three is monotone only until its effective cap reaches three and then freezes. The rank formula can describe that frozen endpoint by replacing k with min(k,3), but increasing generated k beyond three cannot restore a reference whose earliest required rank exceeds three. Interpreting the rank threshold as a model confidence or a semantic correctness guarantee would add an unsupported assumption.")

$ r_(R) (L) = max_(x in R) "rank"_(L) (x) $

$ r(L) = min_(R in cal(R)) r_(R) (L) $

$ S_(k) (L) = bb(1)[k >= r(L)] $

$ S_(k) (P) - S_(k) (V) = cases(1 & p <= k < b, -1 & b <= k < p, 0 & "otherwise") $

= #text("S5. Removed IDs and an exact loss certificate")

#text("Write Bk and Pk for the baseline and promoted prefix sets. A common ID x already in Bk has common-class rank at most its original V rank. Moving the entire common subsequence to the front gives it that common-class rank, never a later one. Thus every baseline-selected common ID remains selected. A noncommon ID retained by Pk has noncommon rank at most k minus the number of common IDs filling the promoted prefix; enough earlier noncommon positions therefore existed in the baseline prefix to contain it. Equivalently, stable promotion cannot add a previously unselected noncommon ID. These statements also hold when k exceeds the union size because both outputs then contain the full union.")

#text("Consequently, removed IDs D=Bk minus Pk are noncommon, and added IDs H=Pk minus Bk are common. The two output sets have equal size, so D and H have equal size. If a baseline-complete reference contains only common IDs, it survives. More generally, a baseline-complete reference survives exactly when it avoids D, because all of its remaining IDs were already in Bk and are retained. This gives a useful necessary risk condition without asserting that common IDs are true or false.")

#text("A full loss occurs if and only if at least one reference was complete in Bk, every such complete reference intersects D, and no reference is complete in Pk. The final condition matters: destroying all previously complete alternatives does not prevent a different alternative from becoming complete using the newly added common IDs. Checking only one preferred annotation would also be inadequate; the loss certificate must quantify over the original family. The saved output stores the relevant removals, additions, and alternatives so a reader can audit this distinction.")

#text("There is a separate universal boundary. If two equal-size output sets differ, choose an ID x present in the baseline and absent from the other output. Under the admissible singleton reference family {{x}}, the baseline passes and the other fails. Therefore, without restrictions on possible references, a same-size transformation that changes the set cannot guarantee full-containment no-regression for every family. This is a quantified annotation-contract statement, not an impossibility result for useful skill integration. It does not say every changed output loses on the actual task, and it does not license choosing a favorable artificial reference after seeing model behavior.")

#text("The universal singleton construction need not satisfy the both-input-sufficient restriction used in S6: the removed noncommon ID may occur in only one input. Its counterexample therefore cannot refute that conditional theorem. The order of quantifiers matters: a guarantee for every reference family is stronger than a guarantee only for families witnessed in both complete inputs.")

$ D = B_k ∖ P_k, quad H = P_k ∖ B_k $

$ D subset.eq (A union B) ∖ C, quad H subset.eq C $

$ |D| = |H| $

= #text("S6. Conditional cap-four theorem and its limits")

#text("The theorem assumes: A and B each contain at most three distinct IDs; each full input contains at least one complete nonempty reference alternative; the complete deduplicated union is formed before truncation; the common and noncommon classes preserve their relative V order; and the endpoint is full-output containment. The two inputs may contain different reference alternatives. This is an annotation condition on full stored lists, not an empirical Q gate on independently authored programs and not a guarantee that each shortened input is sufficient.")

#text("Partition by the common-set size c. If c=0, P=V exactly, so no baseline-success-to-promotion-failure transition is possible at any capacity. This establishes no-loss even if both prefixes fail. If c>=2, the union has at most 3+3-2=4 IDs. At k>=4 both outputs therefore contain the union, and the sufficient-input assumption implies success. If c=1, the union has at most five IDs. At k>=4 the promoted prefix retains the common ID and omits at most one union ID. An omitted noncommon ID belongs to only one input, so the other input is entirely retained; its complete reference alternative remains. These cases exhaust the input class.")

#text("The distinction between success and no-loss is indispensable. Take A=(0,1,2), B=(3,4,5), with alternatives {0,1,2} and {3,4,5}. Both full inputs are sufficient, but at capacity four both orders are (0,3,1,4) and neither alternative is complete. The implication 'baseline passes implies promotion passes' is true because the antecedent is false; unconditional promoted success is false. This is the condition that exposed the initial verifier's overstrong assertion. Likewise, the theorem allows gains: S4's authored common-singleton example has a capacity-four gain. The bank's all-tie result above three is not the theorem's conclusion.")

#text("The input-length restriction is substantive. In a manually derived illustration, let A=(1,2,3,0), B=(4,5,0), with alternatives {1,2,3} and {4,5}. The complete inputs are sufficient and the partition is stable, but one input has length four. V=(1,4,2,5,3,0) at cap four passes via {4,5}; P=(0,1,4,2,5,3) at cap four completes neither alternative. This is an explanatory counterexample outside the theorem's class, not an additional observed task or a rerun of the finite benchmark.")

#text("First-three and exact endpoints remain outside the guarantee. A generated fourth ID can complete a reference that first-three never reads, while exact matching may reject a sufficient superset. Truncating inputs before union formation also changes which alternatives can survive. Empty references are deliberately excluded: admitting an empty valid alternative would make full containment vacuously pass, changing the meaning of qualification and all cap-zero results. Boundary handling is part of the proposition, not a detail to fix after seeing the score.")

= #text("S7. Saved and minimal failure witnesses")

#text("The preserved actual diagnostic witness is scifact_v4_382. A=(1,2,0) contains alternative {1,2}; B=(4,5,0) contains alternative {4,5}. The shared ID is zero. V=(1,4,2,5,0) and P=(0,1,4,2,5). At cap three the baseline retains IDs one and two, whereas promotion removes two and adds zero. It thereby completes neither original alternative. The inputs, union, output count, references, and noncommon order are fixed; common-class priority identifies this particular transition.")

#text("At cap four, promoted (0,1,4,2) again includes {1,2}. Its first-three prefix remains (0,1,4), so generated length and evaluator length tell different stories. At cap five or six, both operators emit all five IDs and full containment passes; first-three still reads the same failing promoted prefix. No claim about the semantic irrelevance of sentence zero is needed for the set calculation. The two recorded losses in the bank are ordered pair views, not two independent trials of real skills.")

#text("The separate authored certificate enumerates ID universes zero through six, all distinct-ID ordered inputs of lengths zero through three whose union is that universe, and one/two-alternative families witnessed in both full inputs. It covers 3,046 input pairs and 122,571 families, including overlapping alternatives, at seven capacities. Lexicographic witness selection records the smallest union size and then total input length where losses occur. This is minimality within the declared exhaustive finite class and selection order, not a global minimal natural-language counterexample.")

#result-table(([#text("Cap / union / total input")],[#text("A ; B")],[#text("Alternatives")],[#text("Baseline ; promoted prefix")],),(([#text("1 / 3 / 4")],[#text("(0,1) ; (1,2)")],[#text("{0} or {1,2}")],[#text("[0] ; ")#link(<bib1>)[#text("[1]")]#text("")],),([#text("2 / 4 / 5")],[#text("(0,1,2) ; (3,1)")],[#text("{0,1,2} or {3}")],[#text("(0,3) ; (1,0)")],),([#text("3 / 5 / 6")],[#text("(0,1,2) ; (3,2,4)")],[#text("{0,1} or {2,3,4}")],[#text("(0,3,1) ; (2,0,3)")],),([#text("0 and 4..6")],[#text("No loss in the declared class")],[#text("Nonempty alternatives")],[#text("No-loss, not unconditional success")],),),"Saved certificate witnesses for the both-input-sufficient class. These authored families are not new independent dataset observations.")

= #text("S8. Paired denominators and weighting reversal")

#text("A pair-capacity transition is a matched comparison: score the baseline and promotion on the same ordered inputs, task references, endpoint, and cap. A gain is a zero-to-one change; a loss is one-to-zero; ties include both failure/failure and success/success. The pair-pooled contrast equals gains minus losses divided by all retained pairs. Dropping unchanged failures, empty outputs, or zero-common pairs changes the denominator and cannot be called the same effect. A separate qualified-history target would additionally need actual independent authoring and an observed Q gate, which are absent.")

#text("The full cohort contains 22,794 ordered pairs nested within 309 tasks and 204 recorded components. Its both-full-input-sufficient stratum contains 12,067 pairs within 308 tasks and 203 components. At low capacities this stratum does not require each input's own truncated prefix to be sufficient. The stratum is selected by auditor references, after the response bank exists; its means cannot be treated as qualification yield or as an unbiased sample of useful skills. Components describe recorded structural grouping, not guaranteed semantic independence.")

#text("For pair-pooled weighting, each pair receives equal mass. For equal-task weighting, first average pair changes within each task, then average tasks. For equal-component weighting, additionally average task means within each component, then average components. These summaries differ whenever pair counts or task counts differ. In the sufficient-input stratum, cap-one gains 565 and losses 1,409 give -844/12067=-0.0699428193 approximately. The predeclared equal-component result is +3363459013/266277211200=+0.0126314190 approximately; the equal-task result is approximately +0.01013. The sign change is descriptive sensitivity to unit weighting, not a numerical inconsistency.")

#text("A new teaching example makes the mechanism transparent without claiming to reproduce the bank: component X has one task with 100 pairs and pair contrast -0.20; component Y has one task with 10 pairs and contrast +0.30. Pair pooling gives (-20+3)/110=-17/110, approximately -0.15455. Equal-component weighting gives (-0.20+0.30)/2=+0.05. Different probability measures answer different questions even on identical rows. The actual bank's exact fractions above are saved results; these two invented components are only an arithmetic explanation.")

#text("Computational size must remain separate from independent sample size. Seven caps create 159,558 pair-capacity contracts, two operators create 319,116 outputs, and three endpoints create 957,348 score cells. None creates an additional task or independent history. A future inferential plan would declare whether histories and source families are fixed or sampled and account for both dependencies. The current exact enumerations support finite descriptive claims without a population confidence interval or power guarantee.")

$ d_t = frac(1, n_t) sum_(i=1)^(n_t) (S(P_(t i)) - S(B_(t i))) $

$ d_("component") = frac(1, |cal(C)|) sum_(c in cal(C)) frac(1, |T_c|) sum_(t in T_c) d_t $

$ d_("pooled") = frac(sum_t n_t d_t, sum_t n_t) $

