= #text("S9. Complete capacity results and endpoint tradeoffs")

#text("The following table restores pass counts and ties that the compact main table omits for space. Every row retains the same 22,794 pairs. At cap one the pooled full-containment change is (565-2700)/22794; at cap two it is (95-25)/22794; at cap three it is -2/22794. These pair-pooled fractions are not the equal-component primary summaries. For example, cap-three equal-component difference is -1/13056, demonstrating why the displayed transition count is not itself the component average.")

#text("The full-containment boundary and the first-three plateau must be read together. For k>=3, first-three evaluates the same three leading IDs at every generated cap, so its zero-gain/two-loss pattern persists through six. Full containment sees newly appended IDs and has no transitions at caps four through six on this bank, although both methods' absolute pass counts rise. Identical scores between methods do not mean all tasks pass: even cap six leaves 2,255 union-unreachable pairs. A complete union cannot create a missing reference ID.")

#text("Exact-annotated changes follow a different pattern: at cap one they coincide with sufficient changes for this bank; at cap two promotion has 66 exact gains and no exact losses; from cap three onward all exact comparisons tie. The contract, not the theorem, determines this pattern. The S4 rank proof is deliberately not cited as an exact-match guarantee. Original annotations may omit semantically valid evidence, so the result is phrased as exact reference agreement rather than truth recovery.")

#text("The inherited agreement-only audit further shows why an apparently favorable score can be misleading. Against left-union, intersection-only has 13,855 sufficient losses while also having 2,328 exact gains and 22 exact losses. Under equal-component weighting it emits sufficient/exact scores 0.290889/0.150539, compared with a pairwise output-length-matched uniform control at 0.356638/0.221823. It outputs fewer IDs, and the matched random control makes the resulting score difference interpretable. None of these proportions is a success rate for newly executed skills.")

