= #text("S10. Strong baselines and restricted selection rights")

#text("Simple controls are strong because they isolate what the proposed signal contributes. Round-robin preserves interleaving without reading references. Stable promotion changes class priority while matching output count. Intersection-only tests abstaining from noncommon material, but must be compared with an exactly matched-length random subset. Left-priority and reversal controls expose input-order dependence. A negative control may outperform a proposed rule without thereby becoming an independently validated deployment policy. All selection rights and candidate membership remain part of the experiment.")

#text("The inherited component-fold selector chooses among exactly four declared rules: left-union, round-robin, agreement-first, and agreement-only. Every sufficient-objective fold selects round-robin; every exact-objective fold selects agreement-only. Thus cross-fitting adds no score through variable selection. Its four-rule per-pair gold oracle reaches 0.840200 sufficient and 0.153696 exact, but the excluded disagreement-first control reaches 0.842416 sufficient. This is not a contradiction: a maximum over one candidate set does not bound a method outside that set. Retrospective admission of the negative control would be a new selection decision.")

#text("Component-held-out choice makes local access rights clear but cannot undo prior exposure of the annotations, tasks, and diagnostic design. Cawley and Talbot ")#link(<bib21>)[#text("[21]")]#text(" is cited only within its recorded metadata/abstract scope; we transfer no unread full-text theorem. Self-consistency ")#link(<bib20>)[#text("[20]")]#text(" is a separate final-answer aggregation mechanism with sampled reasoning paths, not a native implementation of our sentence-ID intersection. ERASER ")#link(<bib4>)[#text("[4]")]#text(" supplies a rationale-evaluation distinction, not proof that an annotation-contained output is faithful to a model. SkillsBench ")#link(<bib19>)[#text("[19]")]#text(" is the versioned release artifact, not a paper performance baseline run here.")

#text("Future native or inspired comparisons need their own explicit contract. Conservative replay must be allowed to reject, narrow, split, or fall back; an always-merge straw controller would suppress its strength. Typed migration needs caller/resource updates and behavioral acceptance beyond schema legality. Runtime routing/assembly must be charged for its decisions and any LLM calls. A fixed-pool adapter and the full native system answer different questions: identical candidate bytes may be appropriate for the first but remove a native system's candidate-generation mechanism from the second. No imported score is placed beside the local finite counts.")

