== #text("Preserved v19: Nested graders do not make qualification changes one-sided")

#text("At budget four, the illustrative single-task gate can admit under sufficient but not first-three on 296 tasks; it can admit under first-three but not sufficient on 295 tasks. These are overlapping task-level possibility counts, not observed gate outcomes or two groups of candidate histories. The asymmetry comes from the four-sentence-only task: first-three never accepts an admissible answer there, so it cannot qualify an improvement under that gate. Thus responsewise leniency does not imply selectionwise leniency. Any study that changes the Q grader must retain admission and overlap denominators, not merely rescore final S on the original selected cohort.")

#text("For the real target, no authoring histories, Q evaluations, eligible pairs or assigned S cells exist. The actual Q overlap, behavioral endpoint-disagreement frequencies and composition effects stay null. The useful new result is the exact separation between what endpoint structure can certify and what only a candidate-history experiment can identify.")

= #text("Preserved v19: Constrained union-replay protocol")

#text("The unrestricted v14 response-space radius permits any admissible deterministic output per arm. It does not describe a learned merger or impose a realizability relation between A, B and AB. We now impose one explicit relation: AB is the ordered deduplicated union of A followed by previously unseen IDs from B. A second operator returns only that union's first three IDs. Neither is a model or a skill implementation; they are mechanistic controls for what a measurement can call a failure.")

#text("We freeze the prior task_measurements.json and diagnostic_rows.jsonl before execution. Every explicit ids, append_ids and prepend_ids field is inspected. The 4,734 explicit responses include oracle original annotations and deliberately constructed diagnostics. Within each task we deduplicate ordered responses and retain only responses of length at most three. This yields 2,494 unique input responses on all 309 evidence-bearing tasks in 204 structural components; no task has an empty retained bank. The restriction deliberately excludes a four-sentence-only original rationale as an isolated full answer. It is an expressibility constraint, not a successful qualification decision.")

#text("All ordered pairs from each task's retained bank are replayed, including A=B and both A,B versus B,A. There are 22,794 pairs and 45,588 operator applications. Ordered pairs are necessary because a ranked-capacity merger prioritizes A. Identical pairs remain because idempotence is part of the declared control. Pair multiplicities are diagnostic design choices, not sampled model-use frequencies. The pair-pooled denominator and the equal-task estimand are therefore reported separately.")

#text("For each endpoint we compute merged score minus the arithmetic mean of the two isolated scores. The primary summary averages first over every task's ordered pairs and then equally over 309 tasks. An equal-component result first averages task summaries inside each of the 204 components. Pair pooling weights tasks with a larger retained response bank more heavily and is a sensitivity summary rather than a replacement for the primary aggregation.")

#text("Input labels are auditor-correct throughout. Each isolated input has at most three sentence IDs; untruncated union can have six, whereas prefix3 has at most three. Thus an untruncated gain is not evidence of superiority at identical output cost. The analysis fixes task identity and label correctness, not realized tokens, model inference or context usage. No Q score or candidate history is generated.")

$ M_(union)(A,B) = "ordered-deduplicate"(A "then" B) $

$ M_(3) (A,B) = "first-three"(M_(union)(A,B)) $

$ d_(g) (A,B) = g(M(A,B)) - frac(g(A)+g(B),2) $

== #text("Preserved v19: Monotone evidence can receive a negative exact-match contrast")

#text("An untruncated union contains every evidence ID returned by either input. Under the sufficient endpoint, any accepted input rationale is therefore still present. We verify zero losses relative to either input on every pair. Nevertheless exact matching is nonmonotone: when A and B contain different individually valid rationale alternatives, their union may equal no original annotation set. Original alternatives [0] and ")#link(<bib1>)[#text("[1]")]#text(" provide the minimal counterexample: both isolated answers are exact, while union [0,1] is sufficient but nonexact.")

#text("The executed equal-task union contrast is +0.199744 for sufficient, +0.136890 for first-three and -0.179878 for exact. Equal-component values are +0.198667, +0.136938 and -0.178035, so the sign reversal does not disappear under that declared regrouping. Pair-pooled values are +0.185838, +0.129815 and -0.206414. These are three different weighting summaries of the same diagnostic objects, not independent replications.")

#text("Exact grading produces 700 ordered pairs whose two isolated responses pass but merged union fails; sufficiency produces zero. Exact union also falls below at least one input in 8,718 pairs. A negative exact contrast is operationally real if exact annotation equality is the task contract. It is insufficient evidence that evidence content was lost, since every merged output is verified to contain both input sets. We do not redefine exact failure away; we restrict its mechanism interpretation.")

#text("The union never beats both isolated responses under sufficiency in this retained bank. Its positive contrast comes from cases with one successful and one unsuccessful input, not observed complementary partial rationales becoming a full answer. This negative coverage fact matters: the existing diagnostic construction does not span every behavior a real merger might exhibit. No frequency in this bank estimates the probability of such complementarity in model outputs.")

